* TR10 PLL blocks -- phase-frequency detector and charge pump.
*
* Classic two-DFF PFD:
*   DFF1: D=1, clock = REF -> UP
*   DFF2: D=1, clock = FB  -> DN
*   reset = UP AND DN, so a pulse on the leading input is reproduced on the
*   matching output and both are cleared once the other edge arrives.  The
*   average (UP-DN) is a linear function of the phase error over +-2*pi and
*   also steers the frequency, so the loop acquires from any starting
*   frequency inside the VCO range.
*
* The DFF is edge triggered (master transparent on the low clock phase,
* slave on the high phase) with an asynchronous reset that pulls both latch
* states low; clearing only the slave would let the still-high master
* re-set it.
*
* Charge pump: resistors (10k / 13.8k) switched into the loop-filter node,
* about 200 uA at vout = 2.9 V.  A current source in series with the switch
* leaves a floating source node whose capacitance swallows the few-ns PFD
* pulses; the resistor has no such node.  The switches are small (20u/40u)
* so the PFD outputs can drive their gates.
*   UP high -> NMOS sinks current (Vtune down, VCO faster)
*   DN high -> PMOS sources current (Vtune up,   VCO slower)
* The polarity matches RX_VCO, whose frequency falls as vtune rises.
*
* Ports:
*   PFD ref fb por up dn dnb vdd vss
*   CP  up dnb vout vdd vss
*
* rgval=-1 (computed ~40 ohm gate R) on the digital devices; rgval=0 is
* clamped to 1e-12 and breaks the DC operating point.
.subckt DFF_R clk rst q qb vdd vss
XI0 clk clkb vdd vss INV
* master: transparent while clk is low, D = 1
XL1 vdd m mb clkb vdd vss LATCH_D
* slave: transparent while clk is high
XL2 m q qb clk vdd vss LATCH_D
* asynchronous reset clears both latches
Xrm m rst vss vss NMOS_nf w=60u l=1u nf=2 rgval=-1
Xrs q rst vss vss NMOS_nf w=60u l=1u nf=2 rgval=-1
.ends DFF_R

.subckt PFD ref fb por up dn dnb vdd vss
XFF1 ref rst up upb vdd vss DFF_R
XFF2 fb  rst dn dnb vdd vss DFF_R
* reset = (UP AND DN) OR ~POR.  por is active low and holds both DFFs
* cleared at power-up; without it the latches power up set and the PFD
* settles into the false DN-wide state.
Xr1 up dn rstb vdd vss NAND2
Xr2 rstb por rst_and vdd vss NAND2
* four inversions keep the polarity and set a reset width of a few ns:
* long enough for the 60u pulldowns to clear both latches, short enough
* not to span the phase difference at 1 MHz
Xr3 rst_and d1 vdd vss INV
Xr4 d1 d2 vdd vss INV
Xr5 d2 d3 vdd vss INV
Xr6 d3 rst vdd vss BUF
.ends PFD

.subckt CP up dnb vout vdd vss
.param rp=10k rn=11.3k
* Resistor-based charge pump.  An ideal current source in series with the
* switch leaves the source node floating: its ~50 fF must swing between
* vdd and vout on every pulse, which swallows the few-ns PFD pulses and
* creates a dead zone.  Resistors have no such node.  rp/rn are chosen for
* ~200 uA at vout = 3 V; the loop feedback absorbs the voltage dependence.
Rp vdd np {rp}
Xsp vout dnb np vdd PMOS_nf w=40u l=1u nf=2 rgval=-1
Xsn vout up nn vss NMOS_nf w=20u l=1u nf=2 rgval=-1
Rn nn vss {rn}
.ends CP
